(project borge-docs-book
 (section book
  (doc "Print the codebase as a paginated book, with a round trip back to the spec via the inbox")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|
     Origin (roerick, 2026-06-27): "I want to print codebases. like
     i want to order them linearly from start to finish, and read them
     like a book. this is literate programming. i want to be able to
     print them out, with page numbers, then scan them back in as
     feedback to the LLM and have it spec changes."
   |) *)
  (* agent note (|
     verified at this commit: print + manifest implemented. build-pass,
     runtest-pass, smoke-pass (borge book print . -> 363-page A4 PDF +
     manifest, line numbers render, deterministic tree_checksum).
     book-absorb remains (status planned) — the long-tail round trip.
   |) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The literate-programming section says the spec is the
        primary artifact and code is derived. The book is the
        physical reading surface for that derived artifact: a
        paginated, linearly ordered rendering of the codebase that
        a human can read away from a screen, mark up by hand, and
        feed back into the spec pipeline.

        The book is NOT a weave. Borge does not tangle code out of
        prose and does not interleave spec and code the way WEB
        does. The spec stays in .borg files; the book is a
        read-only projection of already-written code. What makes
        it literate is that its order is the spec's order: the
        table of contents is the section tree, and each chapter is
        the code a section covers. You read the codebase the way
        it was specified.

        The round trip:

          code -> book (print) -> paper -> inbox (absorb) -> spec

        Three of these four hops already exist. print produces the
        paper. absorb reads marked-up scans back and files inbox
        items; it does NOT edit the spec. The human triages the
        inbox in Plan mode, the same way every other scope concern
        becomes a spec change. A noisy OCR read produces a messy
        inbox item that can be closed, not a corrupted spec.

        Why the book earns its keep even without the round trip:
        reading code on paper, in a fixed linear order, with no
        scrollback and no folding, surfaces structure that the
        editor hides. The absorb side is a bonus, not the
        justification.

        Two render modes, shipped in order:

        1. code-only (MVP). Each page is source with line numbers
           and a right-hand notes column. Ship this first.

        2. woven (later). Interleave each section's spec text with
           the code it covers, chapter by chapter. More work; only
           worth it once code-only proves the book gets opened.

        Scope: a chapter is the code covered by one .borg section.
        Whole-codebase printing is print every chapter in spec
        order, not a separate mode. Design for chapter-scoped
        printing and let the whole book fall out of it.
      |))
  )
  (subsection book-print
   (doc "Render code to a paginated, annotated document")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge book print [DIR] [--root FILE] [-o STEM]

         Linearizes code into a paginated document with page
         numbers, line numbers on every page, and a right-hand
         margin wide enough to write in.

         Ordering is inline-aware (v2): the root project's inline
         tree IS the table of contents. Chapters render depth-first
         in (inline ...) written order — the same order `borge
         inline` prints, and the one order that `book export` and
         the habitat loader consume — so paper, export, and web can
         never disagree about chapter sequence. The root .borg opens
         the book (the spec is the primary artifact per
         literate-programming), then each inlined chapter in the
         order its parent names it.

         Files the walk does not reference — .ml/.mli code and any
         .borg outside the tree — sort lexicographically AFTER the
         inline tree, as an appendix. There is no readdir order
         anywhere in the pipeline: two fresh clones print the same
         book. (The v1 four-tier + dependency-rank ordering is
         retired by this rule; foundations-before-dependents inside
         the appendix is a future refinement, not a v2 requirement.)

         A root whose inline tree fails to build (missing target,
         cycle, parse error) degrades to a lone chapter with a
         stderr warning — the book still renders, deterministically.

         Determinism is byte-level, not just order-level. The walk
         is a pure function of file contents; pdflatex runs inside
         the work directory with SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH and
         FORCE_SOURCE_DATE pinned, so the PDF's creation date and
         /ID derive from a fixed epoch, and listings are cited by
         temp-dir-independent relative paths. Two runs of the same
         tree — in any directory, from any clone — produce
         byte-identical PDFs. Wall clock survives in exactly one
         artifact: the manifest's rendered_at.

         --root FILE selects an exact subtree: the book is the
         inline tree rooted at FILE and nothing else — no appendix,
         no sibling roots. This is the paper-side hook for course
         roots (pricklypear borge/habitat-book.borg
         course-documents): a course .borg ordering chapters prints
         as that course, and a single chapter prints alone. Unlike
         the default walk, which degrades on a broken inline tree
         so the whole book still prints, an explicit selection is a
         contract: a missing file or an unbuildable tree is a hard
         error.

         Still long-tail: the woven view (each chapter's code
         attached to its spec section), page-number cross-references
         between sections, and the --section/--woven flags.

         Rendering target: LaTeX with the listings package — line
         numbers, framed code blocks, and a right-hand marginnote
         column for free; pdflatex to PDF. listings gives proper
         source typography without a custom line-numbering pass.
         groff/ms remains a viable fallback on systems without a
         TeX install (it is in OpenBSD base). The chosen backend
         is a build detail, not a spec invariant. The invariant
         is: paginated, numbered, with a notes column.

         .borg files are NOT rendered as raw sexp. They are parsed
         (Borge_lang.Parse) and emitted as prose: section/subsection
         names as headings, (doc ...) strings as paragraphs, and
         (inline path) as a small note in BOTH registers (the
         printed book carries those chapters next, so inline is
         navigation, not scaffolding). Register is a render mode,
         never a document fork (pricklypear borge/habitat-book.borg
         decision 1): --mode workshop (default) shows the full
         workflow scaffolding — annotated author comments as
         attributed blockquotes, (status X) as an italic marker,
         (verify ...) as one compact scaffold line ("[verify]
         build: ...; smoke: ..."), (untracked path "reason") as a
         small italic note; depends-on and convention stay
         structural noise. --mode reader strips the scaffolding for
         students: prose, headings, and code only. Drift holes stay
         visible in both registers — a file that fails to parse
         falls back to a raw listing either way. The point: the
         spec reads as a document, not as data.

         .ml/.mli files keep the listings treatment (line numbers,
         framed code) — they are code, and the book shows them as code.

         Type coverage (habitat-book workstream): .pp files (the
         Nopales product layer) and .sql files (migrations) are code
         chapters too — same listings treatment, cited under their own
         extension. They are not named by the inline tree, so they
         sort into the lexicographic appendix like the other code.
         Gitignored vault dumps (pp-sync snapshot output, e.g.
         pricklypear backups/) are skipped like build artifacts: they
         are copies of branch state, not source.

         Page furniture, every page:
         - page number (top or bottom corner)
         - chapter name (the section title) and section path
         - line numbers for the source on that page
         - a right-hand notes column, blank, for hand annotations

         Output is a document file (PDF/PS, or text) plus a
         manifest sidecar (see book-manifest) on the same stem:
         foo.book.pdf and foo.book.manifest.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection book-manifest
   (doc "Sidecar mapping page N to file, with a tree checksum. Line numbers are on the page.")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Every book print emits a manifest alongside the document:
         <stem>.book.manifest. Default gitignored (it is a build
         artifact); tracked only if the user wants a durable record.

         The manifest is what makes the absorb round trip possible.
         Line numbers are printed on every page (listings
         numbers=left), so a margin note next to line 127 already
         names its own line — the LLM reads it off the page. The
         manifest only needs to answer "which file is page 14?" so
         a note in the gutter between two files is unambiguous.

         Fields:
         - stem: the document stem the manifest belongs to
         - rendered_at: timestamp of the print run
         - backend: the renderer used (latex-listings, groff-ms, ...)
         - tree_checksum: digest of the exact tree printed (ordered
           file list plus each file's content hash)
         - pages: array of page entries, each with n (page number),
           file (source path on that page), and chapter (section
           title). Line numbers are NOT in the manifest — they are
           printed on the page itself and read directly by absorb.

         tree_checksum is the stale-book guard. On absorb, if the
         working copy's checksum no longer matches the manifest,
         the book is stale relative to the code and the absorb step
         refuses. This stops a scan of a three-week-old book from
         filing inbox items against line numbers that have since
         moved.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection book-absorb
   (doc "Read marked-up scans and file inbox items — never edit the spec")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge book absorb <scan...> [--stem NAME]

         Takes scanned page images (PNG or PDF) of a previously
         printed book, extracts the margin annotations, and files
         one inbox item per annotation. It does NOT edit any .borg
         file. The round trip ends at the inbox, by design — the
         same place every other scope concern lands, and the only
         place a spec change is allowed to start.

         Items conform to the inbox file format (see the inbox
         section). Pipeline per page:

           1. A vision-capable LLM sees the page and returns the
              page number from the header, the printed code on the
              page for grounding, and each margin annotation with
              the line it sits next to and its text.
           2. Map page to file:lines via the manifest.
           3. Resolve the annotation to a .borg section using the
              manifest's section field.
           4. Emit an inbox item:
                source: scope-concern | bug-report | feature-request
                         (best-effort from the note)
                affects: section:"<section path from manifest>"
                filed-by: agent:book-absorb
                doc: the human's annotation, quoted verbatim, plus
                     the file:lines it was anchored to

         Fidelity is the real risk and is owned here, not elsewhere.
         The two hard problems are handwriting legibility and which
         line a scribble refers to. Mitigations, all on the print
         side so the absorb side can stay dumb:
         - structured annotation convention (circle the line, write
           the change intent in the margin) — documented in print
           output, not enforced
         - line numbers on every page (book-print)
         - generous line spacing and a wide notes column (book-print)
         - short intents, not essays (documented convention)

         Safety: absorb never writes .borg. A bad OCR read yields a
         messy inbox item the human can close or edit; it cannot
         corrupt the spec. A --apply bypass that writes spec diffs
         directly is explicitly NOT in scope for v1; the inbox gate
         is the whole point.

         Stale-book guard: if the manifest's tree_checksum does not
         match the working copy, absorb refuses and prints a message
         telling the human to reprint and rescan.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
)
